// Call sites in rekordbox (arm64) that the patched binary routes into librbmods.dylib, by name, so that a
// rekordbox update that moves code doesn't break them. Read by patch_binary.py (to patch the binary) and by
// core (the jump table and the originals); a hook's index is its line order, so after any change here rebuild
// the app with build.sh (not install.sh).
//
//   HOOK(name, caller, nth, count, target, kind)
//     The call site is the nth (from 0) of the count calls to target inside the function caller (both mangled
//     symbols; "stub:_x" is the dyld stub of an imported function). If the caller no longer has count such
//     calls, its code has changed and the patcher leaves the hook out rather than guess.
//     kind -1: the site is "bl <target>"            -> becomes "bl <stub>"
//     kind -2: the site is "b <target>" (tail call) -> becomes "b <stub>" (the hook returns to the caller's caller)
//     kind  n: the site is "adr xn, <target>; nop"  -> becomes "adrp xn, <stub>; add xn, xn, ..."
// The dylib must define extern "C" rbm_hook_<name> with the target's signature; call the original with
// RBM_ORIG(name). A hook left out is never called.
//
//   HOOK_OR(name, caller, nth, count, target, kind)
//     Another site for the hook `name` (a HOOK line above it), used when that one isn't found: where a newer
//     rekordbox has the same call after the code around it moved. It is no hook of its own (no index), the
//     patchers try a hook's sites in order, and the code that includes this file doesn't see it unless it asks.
//
//   HOOK_X86(name, caller, nth, count, target, kind)
//     The hook's site in rekordbox's x86_64 slice (Intel Macs, docs/intel-mac.md), where it isn't the one its HOOK /
//     HOOK_OR lines describe: the compiler orders the same calls differently there (nth), or calls other functions
//     (target, count, kind). A hook's x86_64 sites are its HOOK_X86 lines if it has any (tried in order, like
//     HOOK_OR), else its HOOK / HOOK_OR lines. The kinds in x86_64 code: -1 "call <target>" (E8 rel32), -2 "jmp
//     <target>" (E9 rel32), n >= 0 "lea <any register>, [rip + <target>]" (only the rel32 changes). A hook whose
//     site doesn't exist there gets HOOK_X86(name, "", 0, 0, "", 0): never patched on x86_64. Like HOOK_OR it is no
//     hook of its own, and the code that includes this file doesn't see it unless it asks. tools/dev/hookorder.py
//     checks that every hook's x86_64 site is the same call as its arm64 one.
#ifndef HOOK_OR
#define HOOK_OR(name, caller, nth, count, target, kind)
#define RBM_HOOK_OR_UNUSED
#endif
#ifndef HOOK_X86
#define HOOK_X86(name, caller, nth, count, target, kind)
#define RBM_HOOK_X86_UNUSED
#endif

